n of tit or tet which can even go into a trill and is reminiscent of the equivalent call of the wren which by the way can frequent the same places. Flight and movements adulte sp. Cettia cetti Its flight is low and direct, for example when it moves from one bush to another. Its short wings do not allow for sustained flight over long distances and eastern migrants make night stops when heading to their wintering grounds. Capacité de vol : HWI = 14,3 (vol court, faible dispersion) Behaviour and character adulte sp. Cettia cetti adulte sp. Cettia cetti The presence of the Cetti's Warbler is most often detected by its song. Indeed, it lives hidden in the cover of vegetation and rarely shows itself in the open. Its dull brown plumage does not make the task of the observer easier, even if they are patient! It is particularly difficult to observe, even though it is common in many areas. In shrubs or in reeds, it moves hopping from one stem to another or on the ground, agitated by nervous movements. It often raises its tail. This cettiid is mainly sedentary to the west of its range and can therefore be affected by severe cold winters to the point of disappearing abruptly from a territory, as was the case in northern France in January 1985. Habitat adulte sp. Cettia cetti Cetti's Warbler is found at least in western Europe in densely vegetated wet areas with bushes such as willows, brambles, plum trees, etc. along rivers, pools, marshes, irrigated crops and on the margins of wet woodlands such as alder, poplar, riparian habitats. Phragmites marshes are especially sought. They are always close to water but the tree cover should not be too extensive. Further east, it is also found in more open wetlands with small copses along streams, including at high altitude. Additional data Habitat Freshwater | thickets near water, swampy vegetation, reedbeds | Shrubland Bird et al. 2020, Sibley 1996, Tobias et al. 2021 Densité Semi-open habitats Tobias et al. 2021 Mode de vie DIURNAL | Terrestrial Bird et al. 2020 Altitude 1450 m Diet - feeding behaviour adulte sp. Cettia cetti The Cetti's Warbler is an omnivorous insectivore. It feeds on insects and their larvae, as well as spiders, small molluscs, worms, etc. It looks for food on the ground like a troglodyte or even a small mammal. Additional data Niveau trophique Carnivore Pigot et al. 2020, Tobias et al. 2021 Niche trophique Invertivore Pigot et al. 2020, Tobias et al. 2021 Niche de recherche Generalist Pigot et al. 2020 Alimentation Invertebrate Bird et al. 2020 Breeding and nesting adulte sp. Cettia cetti adulte plum. nuptial sp. Cettia cetti Cetti's Warbler is known for frequently practicing polygamy, particularly where it is common. Females normally make two successive nests. The nest is built from vegetal elements (leaves, dry grass, fibers) and lined internally with feathers, fur and other soft elements such as the fluff of rushes. It is hung in the shrubby and/or herbaceous vegetation (rushes, nettles, etc.) up to a height of 2 meters, often held by a fork. The female lays 3-4 eggs, sometimes 5, of a plain light reddish brown. She alone is responsible for incubation for 16 to 17 days. Young remain in the nest for a bit less than 15 days and are dependent on adults for another fifteen days after they take flight. Additional data âge première reproduction 1 ans Bird et al. 2020 Ponte 4 à 4,8 œufs Bird et al. 2020, Lisleland et al. 2007, Myhrvold et al. 2015 Tentatives par an 1,5 Myhrvold et al. 2015 Parade ground displays only, including displays on trees and bushes Lisleland et al. 2007 Masse de l’œuf 1,8 g Lisleland et al. 2007, Myhrvold et al. 2015 Threats and conservation ♂ adulte plum. nuptial sp. Cettia cetti Statut de conservation IUCN Eteint Menacé Préoccupation mineure Éteint à l'état sauvage Quasi menacé Non évalué EX EW CR EN VU NT LC NE The species is not currently threatened. Pictures View all pictures Data sources Bird et al. 2020 Birdlife's Worldbird Database IOC 12.1 Lislela