('https://www.oiseaux.net/birds/great.reed.warbler.html', 200, "Great Reed Warbler - Acrocephalus arundinaceus - Oiseaux.net Home List Passeriformes Acrocephalidae File Range Pictures Systematics Order Passériformes Family Acrocephalidés Genus Acrocephalus Species arundinaceus Statut de conservation LC Préoccupation mineure Geographic range Couleurs dominantes Morphology Taille 19 à 20 cm Envergure 28 à 29 cm Poids 30 à 30 g 95,4 mm narine\xa0: 12,8 mm L×H\xa0: 4,7 × 5,4 mm 28,1 mm Longévité âge moyen 2,7 ans âge maxi 11.763 ans Other names Rousserolle turdoïde , Carricero tordal , rouxinol-grande-dos-caniços , Drosselrohrsänger , nádirigó , Grote Karekiet , Cannareccione , trastsångare , Trostesanger , trsteniarik veľký , rákosník velký , Drosselrørsanger , rastaskerttunen , Grootrietsanger , balquer , Reyrsöngvari , trzciniak (zwyczajny) , niedru strazds , rakar , Дроздовидная камышевка , ニシオオヨシキリ , 大苇莺 , trastsångare , 西方大葦鶯 , Identification adulte sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus ♂ adulte plum. nuptial sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus The Great Reed Warbler is very similar to the Common Reed Warbler, only in size. It is the largest of all warblers. It looks like a 'magnum' version of the Common Reed Warbler, being one third bigger and twice as heavy - a fact that is clearly apparent in the field. The plumage and bare parts are virtually identical, providing very effective camouflage when in its preferred habitat of reed beds. Both species feed and forage in the same way. In addition to size, a surefire distinguishing factor is the much heavier and proportionate bill of the Great Reed Warbler compared to the Common Reed Warbler. Similar species Marsh Warbler Common Reed Warbler Greater Swamp Warbler Sedge Warbler Manchurian Reed Warbler Oriental Reed Warbler Distribution ♂ adulte plum. nuptial sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus The Great Reed Warbler has a wide distribution range stretching from Spain in the west to China and the Baltic States in the north and North Africa and Iraq in the south. Further east, a similar species, the Eastern Great Reed Warbler, takes its place. Despite this large range, only two subspecies are recognized. Europe is occupied by A. a. arundinaceus. The entire population winters in sub-Saharan Africa in wet and savannah areas. Additional data Migration Full Migrant | Migratory Bird et al. 2020, Tobias et al. 2021 Aire 9336953.31 km² Bird et al. 2020, Tobias et al. 2021 Latitudes de reproduction 23,8 à 63,2 ° Tobias et al. 2021 Voice song and calls ▶ 0:00 / 0:00 ▶ Song 0:59 Grand-Etang Luppy, Moselle, Grand Est (France) Didier Collin CC BY-NC ▶ Call, song 0:35 Sablons, Isère, Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes (France) Vincent Palomares CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Song 1:20 Lattes, Hérault, Occitanie (France) Olivier Swift CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Song 0:45 Piney, Aube, Champagne-Ardenne (France) Fernand Deroussen CC BY-NC-ND ▶ Song 0:35 Este de Lleida en cultivos humedos (Spain) Manuel Grosselet CC BY-NC-ND ▶ Song 0:33 Bwabwata (Namibia) Franck Brunelière CC BY-NC-ND ▶ Song 1:26 Courtenay, Isère, Auvergne Rhône-Alpes (France) Benjamin Drillat CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Call 0:24 Rogliano, Haute-Corse, Corse (France) Stanislas Wroza CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Call 0:36 Brindavan River Camp, Zambezi River (Zambia) Rory Nefdt CC BY-NC-ND All sounds (24) ▶ Recording 0:20 Franck Brunelière ▶ Recording 0:15 Jean-Claude Roché ▶ Song 1:40 Etang de la Mutche, Moselle, Lorraine (France) Didier Collin CC BY-NC-ND ▶ Song 1:06 Gmina Białowieża, Hajnówka County, Podlaskie Voivodeship (Poland) Fernand Deroussen CC BY-NC-ND ▶ Song 0:17 Lattes, Hérault, Languedoc-Roussillon Midi-Pyrénées (France) Olivier Swift CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Call 0:19 Lattes, Hérault, Languedoc-Roussillon Midi-Pyrénées (France) Olivier Swift CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Song 0:45 House of Nature near Lattes, Hérault, Occitanie (France) Manuel Grosselet CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Song 1:26 House of Nature near Lattes, Hérault, Occitanie (France) Manuel Grosselet CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Song 0:59 House of Nature near Lattes, Hérault, Occitanie (France) Manuel Grosselet CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Song 1:49 House of Nature near Lattes, Hérault, Occitanie (France) Manuel Grosselet CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Call, flight call 0:02 Doñana National Park near Almonte, Huelva, Andalucía (Spain) Stanislas Wroza CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Song 2:17 Arles, Bouches-du-Rhône, Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur (France) Benjamin Drillat CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Call 0:09 Sablons, Isère, Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes (France) Vincent Palomares CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Call 3:49 Rogliano, Haute-Corse, Corse (France) Stanislas Wroza CC BY-NC-SA ▶ Call 0:53 Rogliano, Haute-Corse, Corse (France) Stanislas Wroza CC BY-NC-SA ♂ adulte plum. nuptial sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus The song of the Great Reed Warbler, most often emitted from a dry reed stem of Phragmite, is distinct and allows for an immediate identification. Compared to that of other warblers, it is much more powerful, correlating to the bird's size, and is carried far. It is composed of expositions on a relatively slow rhythm and of varying tones. The cracking syllables alternate with others that are higher and shrill. The cries are also correlated with its size. The most frequent is a low and gruff rolling cry. Flight and movements adulte sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus Capacité de vol : HWI = 28,6 (vol moyen, dispersion locale) Behaviour and character adulte sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus ♂ adulte sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus The first migrants are back in April in the south of the area, more likely in May in the north. From this moment on, the reed beds become animated with the territorial and sexual pursuits between adults, punctuated by rough vocal emissions. Later, with the progress of nesting, the bird becomes more discreet, skilfully moving through the reeds. Only the cries of the young betray the presence of the families. A few low flights towards the nearby bushes can be observed. In summer, only a few cries of worry can be heard upon approach and very soon, from July-August, the birds start their post-nuptial migration, largely going unnoticed. Their stay will have lasted only 3-4 months. Habitat ♂ adulte sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus The Great Reed Warbler is the one with the most restricted habitat of all the Warblers, namely exclusively reed beds. It occupies the reed beds bordering water bodies or water courses. Unlike the Reed Warbler which can occupy dry reed beds, the Great Reed Warbler needs permanent water. Despite its size, it is sometimes satisfied by small patches of reeds as well as small lines of reeds along ditches, drains or canals, provided they are in water. Additional data Habitat Reedbeds | Freshwater | Wetland Bird et al. 2020, Tobias et al. 2021, agric.ncape.gov.za Densité Open habitats Tobias et al. 2021 Mode de vie DIURNAL | Terrestrial Bird et al. 2020 Diet - feeding behaviour adulte sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus The Great Reed Warbler feeds mainly on insects (diptera, lepidoptera, ephemeroptera, small odonates,...) but also other arthropods such as spiders, and even on occasion small aquatic vertebrates (small fish and amphibians). Additional data Niveau trophique Carnivore Tobias et al. 2021 Niche trophique Invertivore Tobias et al. 2021 Alimentation Invertebrate Bird et al. 2020 Breeding and nesting juvénile sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus She is comparable to that of the Great Reed Warbler, all proportions kept. The voluminous nest of dry grasses is attached to the stems of reeds from the previous year, above the water. Only one brood is raised and the first departures follow quickly after the emancipation of the young. Additional data âge première reproduction 1,1 ans Bird et al. 2020 Ponte 4 à 4,5 œufs Bird et al. 2020, Lisleland et al. 2007, Myhrvold et al. 2015 Incubation 14,5 jours Myhrvold et al. 2015 Envol 16 jours Myhrvold et al. 2015 Tentatives par an 1,5 Myhrvold et al. 2015 Parade both ground and non-acrobatic flight displays Lisleland et al. 2007 Masse de l’œuf 3,1 g Lisleland et al. 2007, Myhrvold et al. 2015 Threats and conservation adulte plum. nuptial sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus adulte sp. Acrocephalus arundinaceus Statut de conservation IUCN Eteint Menacé Préoccupation mineure Éteint à l'état sauvage Quasi menacé Non évalué EX EW CR EN VU NT LC NE The Great Reed Warbler is experiencing a decline in many places, such as France. However, tracking it in Lorraine has shown only significant fluctuations between years. It is evident that this great trans-Saharan migrant is affected by the prevailing conditions, especially the climate, both on its migratory paths and in its subsaharan wintering grounds and these are responsible, accompanied by mortality, for determining the species' demographics. The health of its breeding areas is the second most important factor, just as it has been shown for the Grasshopper Warbler. Pictures View all pictures Data sources Bird et al. 2020 IOC 12.1 Lisleland et al. 2007 Myhrvold et al. 2015 S. M. Billerman, B. K. Keeney, P. G. Rodewald, & T. S. Schulenberg (Eds.). (2022). Birds of the World. Cornell Laboratory of Ornithology. https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/home Tobias et al. 2021 africanbirdclub.org agric.ncape.gov.za www.birdsofmelanesia.net Further references Birdlife IUCN Red List — IUCN Red List of Threatened species, IUCN GBIF — GBIF - Global Biodiversity Information Facility, GBIF Secretariat Wikidata — Wikidata, la base de connaissances libre, Wikimedia Foundation Encyclopedia of Life — Encyclopedia of life, Encyclopedia of Life NCBI Taxonomy — NCBI Taxonomy Database, National Center for Biotechnology Information eBird — eBird, Cornell Lab of Ornithology et National Audubon Society Birds of the World — Birds of the World, The Cornell Lab of Ornithology TaxRef (INPN) — Inventaire national du patrimoine naturel (INPN), MNHN - INPN Xeno-Canto Original French text by Jean François ( 17/09/2015 ), reviewed by Frédéric Ciszek and Didier Bats on 17/09/2015 Translated by Oiseaux.net AI on 25/07/2023 © 1996-2026 Oiseaux.net")
